title: $:/plugins/crosseye/json-convert/usage
caption: Usage

! Using an importer

An //importer// is a small plugin somebody built for one kind of JSON —
invoices, a reading list, a course export — and gave to you.  It adds a
sidebar tab named for that data.  Everything about //how// the JSON
turns into tiddlers was decided when the importer was made; your part
is four steps.

# ''Get the JSON in.''  Paste it into the ''Source JSON'' box, drop the file on the box, or use the file chooser beside it.  A status line under the box tells you at once whether the text is valid JSON, and if not, where it goes wrong.  If the importer ships a sample, ''Try it with the sample'' loads one so you can watch the whole thing work first.
# ''Convert.''  The big green button (it may be labelled for the data, e.g. ''Stage books'') runs the conversion.  Nothing is written to the wiki yet.  The ''Results'' table counts what was produced and lists any errors, warnings and title collisions.
# ''Review.''  The ''Staging'' list shows every tiddler about to be written.  Expand a row to see its fields and text.  Titles that already exist in the wiki appear under ''Conflicts'': choose ''skip'', ''overwrite'' or ''rename'' for each, or set them all at once.  New titles default to ''add''.
# ''Apply.''  Writes the tiddlers you kept, then clears the staging area.  ''Imported this session'' lists what was written, with links, until you reload.  ''Cancel'' throws the staged tiddlers away instead.

!! Where things go

The importer keeps its working data — the pasted text, the results, the
staged tiddlers and the session list — under `$:/temp/json-convert/`
followed by the importer's own name.  Nothing there is saved with the
wiki, and two importers in one wiki never see each other's data.  Only
''Apply'' writes ordinary tiddlers.

!! What you cannot change here

The profile (which fields become which), any input flattening, and the
default action for a title collision are fixed inside the importer.
That is the point: the person who built it did the hard part once.

!! When it does not fit

An importer is built for one shape of JSON.  When yours differs, the
''Results'' panel says so in plain words rather than staging nothing
quietly:

* ''Nothing was staged'' names the step of the records path that matched nothing, and what the document had there instead.
* ''Empty titles'' says which binding produced them and what the first such record looked like.
* ''This JSON doesn't look like what this importer expects'' lists the fields the importer was built for that your records lack, and any whose type changed, when the importer ships a sample to compare against.

Each of these ends with the same sentence: if your JSON is what it
should be, the importer's profile needs updating by whoever made it, and
the panel links to the studio it came from.  There is nothing to fix on
your side.

!! Building your own

The [[JSON Convert Studio|$:/plugins/crosseye/json-convert-studio]]
plugin adds the Console, a form-based profile editor with pickers, and
the full reference guide.  Install it alongside this runtime to write
profiles and, in due course, generate importers of your own.
